WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:07.629
This is Lakhi Ram Lohar.

00:00:08.255 --> 00:00:11.341
His job was to spray pesticide

00:00:11.341 --> 00:00:14.052
at a tea garden in West Bengal, India.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.347
Lakhi was a single father

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:19.558
to his only daughter, Sugi.

00:00:20.976 --> 00:00:24.396
One day in 2007,

00:00:24.437 --> 00:00:25.772
Sugi went missing.

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:32.362
Lakhiram went to the local police
to file a missing persons report,

00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:37.075
but he was unable to do so
because he was illiterate.

00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:43.081
He didn't have Sugi's birth certificate
or any of her photographs.

00:00:44.541 --> 00:00:47.377
So, the police didn't believe him,

00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:48.545
and did nothing.

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:54.551
He tried, again and again, but no luck.

00:00:55.927 --> 00:00:58.179
It was as if Sugi never existed.

00:00:59.848 --> 00:01:01.558
Ten years later,

00:01:01.558 --> 00:01:05.604
a local nonprofit
that fights human trafficking

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:06.771
took up her case.

00:01:07.772 --> 00:01:09.816
They approached Sugi's school

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:12.944
and requested proof that she had attended.

00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:18.825
Here is the certificate, issued in 2017,

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:22.037
10 years after her disappearance.

00:01:23.204 --> 00:01:24.664
It bothered me

00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:29.628
that the disappearance of a poor girl
doesn't matter to anyone,

00:01:29.628 --> 00:01:30.837
except her family.

00:01:33.048 --> 00:01:34.883
I'm a photojournalist,

00:01:34.924 --> 00:01:39.054
and I report on critical human rights
and social issues,

00:01:39.054 --> 00:01:41.556
not just in my country, India,

00:01:41.598 --> 00:01:43.224
but across the Global South.

00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:46.936
I see stories like this everywhere.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.859
One of the biggest myths
about human trafficking

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:55.862
is that it is a developing world problem.

00:01:56.404 --> 00:02:00.533
But the reality is that human trafficking

00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.412
is a 150-billion-dollar
industry, globally.

00:02:04.454 --> 00:02:09.209
The International Labour
Organization's 2016 report

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:15.590
reveals that 40.3 million people
were trafficked into modern-day slavery,

00:02:15.632 --> 00:02:20.261
out of which 23 million
were women and girls.

00:02:20.845 --> 00:02:25.517
It's as if girls and women
from poor families

00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:27.435
don't have any value,

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:30.313
and their existence or their deaths ...

00:02:31.272 --> 00:02:32.440
don't matter.

00:02:32.941 --> 00:02:36.820
Their stories are mostly
unseen and unheard.

00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:42.325
But it's important to see these stories
and not look away.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.620
Photographs inspire compassion

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.748
and enhance our understanding
of the world.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.627
It is a powerful tool
for making connections with people.

00:02:53.128 --> 00:02:57.090
But when we undertake the task
of sharing people's lives,

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.342
we have an added responsibility.

00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:03.763
Are we representing them
with dignity and fairness?

00:03:03.805 --> 00:03:07.642
Are we telling the whole story
or just a part of it?

00:03:08.184 --> 00:03:13.356
I'm very conscious about stereotypes
and how I represent people.

00:03:14.065 --> 00:03:19.028
Life can be harsh for poor girls
from these countries.

00:03:19.946 --> 00:03:22.615
Boys are given preferential treatment,

00:03:22.657 --> 00:03:26.077
while girls are expected
to do household chores --

00:03:26.119 --> 00:03:28.955
cook, clean, fetch water,

00:03:28.997 --> 00:03:31.166
and take care of their younger siblings.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.171
Often, parents tend to marry
their daughters early

00:03:36.212 --> 00:03:39.299
so they would become
someone else's responsibility.

00:03:40.341 --> 00:03:44.596
Basic education is free
in many developing countries,

00:03:44.637 --> 00:03:48.349
but students still need
to buy books and uniforms,

00:03:48.391 --> 00:03:53.563
and when putting even two meals
on the table is a struggle,

00:03:53.605 --> 00:03:56.274
schools can seem like a luxury.

00:03:57.317 --> 00:04:01.279
So when I went to Nepal a few years ago,

00:04:01.321 --> 00:04:05.200
to document child marriage
for Human Rights Watch,

00:04:05.241 --> 00:04:09.704
I thought the girls I would meet
were forced by their parents to marry.

00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:12.791
What I saw was a bit different.

00:04:12.791 --> 00:04:16.836
The girls I met wanted
to escape a grim situation

00:04:16.878 --> 00:04:19.839
and gain some control of their lives.

00:04:20.507 --> 00:04:22.550
When Tilmaya was 11,

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:24.594
her father took her out of school

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:28.431
and sent her to Pokhara
to work as a domestic maid.

00:04:28.473 --> 00:04:31.184
When she was 15, she eloped

00:04:31.184 --> 00:04:34.354
and married a 20-year-old man
from her village.

00:04:35.146 --> 00:04:36.648
This is Sharmila.

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:40.360
Sharmila's parents tried
to separate her from her boyfriend,

00:04:40.401 --> 00:04:42.153
so she ran away with him.

00:04:42.195 --> 00:04:44.781
She was 14, he was 18.

00:04:45.281 --> 00:04:47.033
When I took this image of her,

00:04:47.075 --> 00:04:49.410
Sharmila was seven months pregnant.

00:04:50.161 --> 00:04:54.874
She said she regrets
leaving school and marrying early

00:04:54.874 --> 00:05:00.004
and that she had no knowledge
of reproductive health or family planning.

00:05:00.547 --> 00:05:06.135
So why would girls leave the safety
of their families,

00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:07.387
drop out of school?

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:09.848
For nothing?

00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:13.268
To gain some control

00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:15.478
of their future.

00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:20.024
At least, in this case, they were able
to choose their own partner.

00:05:20.942 --> 00:05:25.697
I try to dig deep and show
the nuances of the situations,

00:05:25.738 --> 00:05:30.451
because often, poor women and girls
are stripped of their equity and dignity.

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:37.292
I would not have taken these photographs
if the girls didn't want me to.

00:05:38.251 --> 00:05:42.171
And I work very discreetly,
keeping a low profile,

00:05:43.339 --> 00:05:46.342
and go to great lengths
to avoid attention.

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:49.137
I carry minimum gear

00:05:49.178 --> 00:05:51.472
and this dirty-looking tote bag,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.559
which my mother hates.

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:56.519
(Laughter)

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:58.938
The idea is to blend with the crowd.

00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:02.567
Even if I dress simply,

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:04.903
it's understood I am from the city.

00:06:05.612 --> 00:06:09.240
So I tell people I'm a researcher
into female hygiene.

00:06:10.491 --> 00:06:13.745
I started carrying
sanitary napkins in my purse.

00:06:15.288 --> 00:06:16.789
Men are so embarrassed,

00:06:17.832 --> 00:06:19.083
they leave me alone.

00:06:19.125 --> 00:06:22.211
(Laughter)

00:06:22.211 --> 00:06:25.048
(Applause)

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.759
The women offer me tea,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.470
and I sit and chat with them for hours,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.305
until they get bored of me.

00:06:32.847 --> 00:06:35.099
That's when my real work begins.

00:06:35.892 --> 00:06:41.397
The family knows there will be no gossip
or any unwelcome scrutiny,

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:44.192
as I had already been
to 10 different houses.

00:06:45.234 --> 00:06:50.573
Everyone in the village just assumes
I was there to speak about menstruation.

00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:55.453
I'm very careful that my work
does not disrupt

00:06:55.453 --> 00:06:58.373
the lives of the people I photograph,

00:06:58.414 --> 00:07:01.834
while I'm there and long after I've left.

00:07:02.585 --> 00:07:06.798
I do hours of research before diving
into a new territory,

00:07:06.798 --> 00:07:08.633
honoring people's culture,

00:07:08.675 --> 00:07:10.385
abiding by local laws.

00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:12.929
Asking permission,

00:07:12.971 --> 00:07:17.850
seeking informed consent
and not leading with my camera

00:07:17.892 --> 00:07:19.936
has been the foundation of my work.

00:07:20.937 --> 00:07:25.858
And this has helped me overcome
many hostile situations.

00:07:27.026 --> 00:07:30.738
While working on a project
called "Not My Shame,"

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.034
documenting sexual violence
in my country, India,

00:07:35.034 --> 00:07:39.080
I met a young girl I’ll call Beena.

00:07:39.956 --> 00:07:46.421
Beena was abducted and repeatedly raped
by a distant relative.

00:07:47.714 --> 00:07:52.468
This man was also a very powerful
person in the community.

00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:57.181
While I was sitting
with Beena at her house,

00:07:57.181 --> 00:07:59.976
a huge crowd had gathered outside.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.063
Hearing loud voices, I went out,

00:08:04.063 --> 00:08:07.358
only to be surrounded
by a group of hostile men.

00:08:08.651 --> 00:08:11.946
The men snatched my camera bag,

00:08:11.946 --> 00:08:13.531
tore off my purse,

00:08:13.573 --> 00:08:15.158
and started berating me.

00:08:16.451 --> 00:08:22.081
My first thought was to protect Beena
and her family, and also myself.

00:08:23.041 --> 00:08:25.501
I tried to appear calm,

00:08:26.627 --> 00:08:28.087
although I was scared.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.340
The men said they thought I was there ...

00:08:33.009 --> 00:08:35.011
to disrupt their lives

00:08:35.053 --> 00:08:37.764
and spoil the village's reputation.

00:08:39.015 --> 00:08:44.353
I told them I was there to learn
about their lives and culture.

00:08:45.521 --> 00:08:48.483
By the end of our conversation,
an hour later,

00:08:48.524 --> 00:08:51.819
these same men offered me tea and snacks.

00:08:53.404 --> 00:08:55.531
Despite this incident,

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:59.285
Beena and her family
wanted me to share her story

00:08:59.285 --> 00:09:00.453
with the world.

00:09:00.912 --> 00:09:02.872
Beena’s story matters,

00:09:02.914 --> 00:09:04.624
so does Sugi's,

00:09:04.665 --> 00:09:08.086
and hundreds of women's
and girls' like them.

00:09:09.003 --> 00:09:12.924
Their stories would not
have been seen or heard

00:09:12.965 --> 00:09:15.968
if people like me didn't show them.

00:09:17.011 --> 00:09:19.388
We need to start valuing them.

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:24.685
The first step to any change

00:09:24.727 --> 00:09:27.605
is acknowledgment and awareness.

00:09:28.815 --> 00:09:32.026
Otherwise, this cycle of harm
will continue.

00:09:34.195 --> 00:09:38.407
These women and girls exist.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:43.579
And the reason I'm telling you this

00:09:43.621 --> 00:09:47.208
is because we were lucky enough to be born

00:09:47.208 --> 00:09:49.669
in very comfortable circumstances.

00:09:50.878 --> 00:09:52.755
We won the birth lottery.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:55.967
These women have values.

00:09:56.884 --> 00:09:58.052
They exist.

00:09:59.095 --> 00:10:02.765
The least we can do is honor them

00:10:03.724 --> 00:10:04.934
and not look away.

00:10:05.852 --> 00:10:07.019
Thank you.

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:14.026
(Applause)

